-- SPEC §27 at the surface, the twin of the kernel program `cancel-reentered` (`bcc kernel interp
-- cancel-reentered`, Core's `progReT`; its core text is `corpus/core-fin-cancel-reentered.bc`): the
-- cancelled region holds a second handler of `Ex3`, `h3`, around the `op1` it suspends at; the
-- cleanup's `ex3` is outside it and resolves to `catch3`. The kernel program re-enters one name
-- (`3`); the surface gives every site a name of its own, so `h3`'s is not `catch3`'s, and the raise
-- goes to the live outer handler by its own name. The error is kept, re-raised at the canceller,
-- and caught by `catch3`.
--
-- Answer: inr (), and `live 0`. A wrong implementation that delivers the error to the dead inner
-- instance, or drops it, answers `inl ()`.

effect Ex3 { ctl ex3() -> never }
effect Op1 { ctl op1() }

handler catch3: Ex3, 1 -> 1 + 1 = { return(v) = inl v; ex3() = inr () }
handler canc: Op1, 1 -> 1 = { return(v) = v; op1(k) = () }
handler h3: Ex3, 1 -> 1 = { return(v) = v; ex3() = () }

main -> 1 + 1 = with catch3 { with canc { x <- (with h3 { op1() }) unwind (ex3()); x } }
